WEBVTT

00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:03.889
An intact surface can conceal deterioration.

00:00:05.631 --> 00:00:07.968
Here the cut face contains sound timber.

00:00:08.905 --> 00:00:12.628
In bending, fibres farther from the centre have greater leverage.

00:00:13.566 --> 00:00:17.207
The location of damage therefore matters, as well as its size.

00:00:19.067 --> 00:00:21.431
Decay weakens wood without leaving a hole.

00:00:22.773 --> 00:00:27.188
This section has central decay, becoming less severe away from its source.

00:00:28.926 --> 00:00:33.083
The distribution is prescribed: an illustrative material penalty, not

00:00:33.097 --> 00:00:34.850
a forecast of decay growth.

00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:40.045
The weakened region is now nearer the outside.

00:00:41.786 --> 00:00:45.672
The coloured section shows beam utilisation in the remaining material.

00:00:46.610 --> 00:00:47.982
This case is fixed.

00:00:48.920 --> 00:00:52.846
Position changes the bending response; this view cannot demonstrate

00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:54.028
every damage pattern.

00:00:55.700 --> 00:00:57.711
A cavity removes wood completely.

00:00:59.453 --> 00:01:02.347
The cut face shows the shell left around the opening.

00:01:03.284 --> 00:01:05.730
Its size and position affect resistance.

00:01:06.667 --> 00:01:11.056
Thin shells may split or buckle; those failure mechanisms are not predicted

00:01:11.070 --> 00:01:12.551
by this beam bending model.
